    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Data basics</p>
    <h1>Bits and Bytes: Why Internet Speeds Look Different From File Sizes</h1>
    <p class="lead">Files are measured in bytes, but internet speeds are measured in bits. Eight bits make one byte. This guide explains why the two numbers look different, and how to compare them.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Bits and bytes</h2>
<p>A bit holds a single 0 or 1. Eight bits make one byte. A file of 100 megabytes contains 800 megabits.</p>
<h2>Why speeds use bits</h2>
<p>Network speeds have long been measured in bits per second, which is a convention rather than a technical need. When you see a 100 Mbps connection, the best case is about 12.5 megabytes per second.</p>
<h2>Converting</h2>
<ul>
<li>To go from megabytes to megabits, multiply by 8.</li>
<li>To go from megabits per second to megabytes per second, divide by 8.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Decimal and binary sizes</h2>
<p>Storage makers often use 1 KB = 1,000 bytes, while some systems use 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes. The difference is small at kilobytes but adds up at gigabytes, which is why a drive can seem smaller than advertised.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/data-size-converter.html">File Size Converter</a> shows any size in bits, bytes, KB, MB, GB and TB, in decimal or binary units.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>A 25 MB file on a 20 Mbps connection: 25 MB × 8 = 200 megabits, and 200 ÷ 20 = 10 seconds. The same file on a 100 Mbps connection takes 2 seconds, and on a 1 Gbps connection it takes 0.2 seconds.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Why is my download slower than my internet speed suggests?</summary><p>Speeds are quoted in megabits, while file sizes are in megabytes. A 100 Mbps line moves about 12.5 megabytes per second at best, and real downloads are often slower still.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is 1 Mbps the same as 1 MB per second?</summary><p>No. 1 Mbps is 0.125 megabytes per second, so 8 Mbps is about 1 MB per second.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is a gigabit connection in megabytes?</summary><p>One gigabit per second is 1,000 megabits, which is about 125 megabytes per second at best.</p></details>
      </section>
